 ## Background
-The motivation for this renderer stems from the domain of volumetric data obtained by voxelization of complex models defined by implicit functions [S1]. Such models, especially those with sharp or small details, require dense sampling, resulting in huge grids of $1000^3$ and more voxels [S1]. However, since only the surfaces of the objects are typically of interest, it is sufficient to fully represent only voxels close to the surfaces, while compressing the rest [S1]. The technique builds on RLE compression methods described by Novotný [S1]. It was implemented as part of the VRE volume rendering engine, detailed in a paper by Parulek et al. [S1].
+The motivation for this renderer stems from the domain of volumetric data obtained by voxelization of complex models defined by implicit functions [S1]. Such models, especially those with sharp or small details, require dense sampling, resulting in huge grids of $1000^3$ and more voxels [S1]. However, since only the surfaces of the objects are typically of interest, it is sufficient to fully represent only voxels close to the surfaces, while compressing the rest [S1]. The technique builds on RLE compression methods described in the literature [S1]. It is implemented within a GPU-based volume rendering engine using a fragment shader written in GLSL [S1].
 
